  <Fragment slot="lede">Every org chart is a bet about how your company builds things. Place it on purpose.</Fragment>

  <h2>The short answer</h2>

  <p>Begin with the result you want, then draw the chart. Find where work gets stuck on its way to customers, and organise to unstick it. Count more than product, design and engineering: research, product operations, data and product marketing all decide how well value ships. If you're reorganising, write down why, show your reasoning, and be clear about what stays the same.</p>

  <h2>Treat the org chart as a bet</h2>

  <p>Look at an org chart and it's making a claim: this arrangement of people is our best guess at how to get products out and keep them healthy. Guesses can be wrong.</p>

  <p>I've been through a dozen or so reorgs. People are the hard part. How we organise is one of those things at work that almost nobody stops to question.</p>

  <p>It helps to treat the org itself as a product, something designed to help people do a job. Then the first question is obvious: what job?</p>

  <p>Don't go looking for the perfect structure. Organisations grow more like gardens than get built like houses, and they have a habit of doing their own thing.</p>

  <h2>Why reorgs often fail</h2>

  <p>Most reorgs are triggered by cost cutting or a merger, and neither is a neutral starting point. The reorg turns into a power play, or a quick fix that ignores the long term. Customers rarely notice any difference, and that's the good outcome.</p>

  <p>Even well-meant reorgs get bogged down in sign-offs and fear of getting it wrong. The people at the top don't explain their thinking. Everyone else is left confused, cynical or worried about their job.</p>

  <p>That has a real cost. <a href="https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/02678373.2015.1136710" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Studies link reorganisation to worse wellbeing</a> for employees, <a href="https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2016/02/160201220120.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">often even when nobody loses their job</a>. When reorgs help, it's mostly because people end up with <a href="https://oshwiki.eu/wiki/Protecting_worker_health_during_restructuring" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">more autonomy</a>.</p>

  <h2>Signs of a good reorg</h2>

  <p>Write the goal down first. Org design is a design problem, and design needs an intent everyone can work towards. A goal statement might read:</p>

  <blockquote><p>After this change, we deliver value to customers and to each other better than before, and we can keep it up. We get there through clearer communication, a shared sense of purpose at every level, and better ways of sharing context and results across teams.</p></blockquote>

  <p>Then work back from it. Start with the people and weigh that against what the business needs. Look for the friction between your teams and customer value, and between your teams and business results, and organise around those two.</p>

  <p>Four questions to settle the tradeoffs:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>What are we trying to improve by moving people? Why?</li>
    <li>What should be true once the reorg is done?</li>
    <li>Who and what does it take to get value to customers, and how could we line up behind that better?</li>
    <li>What are we deliberately leaving alone for now?</li>
  </ol>

  <p><a href="https://www.atlassian.com/blog/teamwork/what-is-conways-law-acmi" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Conway's Law</a> says your product ends up shaped like your org. If customers shouldn't see the joins, your teams shouldn't trip over them either. Org design isn't only HR's job. It's a product leadership decision.</p>

  <h2>Look beyond the trio</h2>

  <p>Product, design and engineering built modern product management. A product org at scale leans on many more people: user research, product operations, research operations, data and analytics, product marketing, and people and talent.</p>

  <p>A product leader should have a view on how all of those fit together into a team that ships reliably. Owning the software isn't enough. You also own the organisation that produces it.</p>

  <h2>Culture comes before structure</h2>

  <p>Culture starts with what people believe, shows up in the systems they work inside, and ends in what they do. Redraw the chart without touching beliefs or systems, and very little changes.</p>

  <p>Teams that beat their competitors tend to share one trait: <a href="https://rework.withgoogle.com/guides/understanding-team-effectiveness/steps/foster-psychological-safety/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">psychological safety</a>. Lots of leaders talk about it. Far fewer know how to build it. It comes from what leaders do, day to day.</p>

  <p>Hiring is the moment you shape culture on purpose. Be precise about the skills, technical and human, that the role needs, design out as much bias as you can, and look for what someone adds to the culture, not how well they fit it.</p>

  <h2>When to bring someone in</h2>

  <p>If the product org has outgrown its shape, or a reorg is coming and nobody senior owns the design, that's a product leadership gap. A <a href="/fractional/">fractional product leader</a> can write the goal statement, design the structure with the team, and hand it over.</p>

